D["ch15"] = dict(essay="""Chowder is the book's exhale. We have just come through a chapel full of cenotaphs, a sermon about Jonah, a night in bed with a cannibal, and a leaky schooner—and now Melville sits us down and feeds us. I find I trust a writer more when he can be this happy about a bowl of soup. The recipe paragraph ("small juicy clams, scarcely bigger than hazel nuts") is written with the same attention he will later give to the whale's skeleton, and that is the point: the same eye that will be terrified by the whiteness of the whale can also be delighted by pounded ship biscuit.

But notice the chapter's first gesture isn't warmth. It's Ishmael staring at a top-mast that looks like a gallows, counting the two remaining horns, "one for Queequeg, and one for me." He is doing what he did with the Coffin at the Spouter-Inn: reading omens into furniture. Then Mrs. Hussey says "Clam or Cod?" and the omen collapses into a comedy of miscommunication. That is the book's rhythm in miniature—dread, then a joke, then a meal.

The thing I keep coming back to is the harpoon rule. Young Stiggs, back from an "unfort'nt v'y'ge" with only three barrels of oil, was found dead with his harpoon in his side. Mrs. Hussey turns a suicide into a house policy about weapons in bedrooms, the way institutions always convert grief into procedure. And it sets up the next chapter's locked-door panic almost too neatly. Melville is planting; I only see it on the second read.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="A sort of crick was in my neck as I gazed up to the two remaining horns; yes, _two_ of them, one for Queequeg, and one for me.", note="Ishmael cannot look at any object without reading his own death into it. What's funny is how bodily it is—a crick in the neck from staring up at a gallows. The book will keep asking whether omens are real or just what a nervous mind does with a top-mast."),
 dict(quote="Oh, sweet friends! hearken to me.", note="The tone of a preacher, borrowed for a chowder recipe. Father Mapple used almost this register two chapters ago about Jonah. Melville is telling you, gently, that the sacred and the delicious run on the same voltage in this book."),
 dict(quote="Chowder for breakfast, and chowder for dinner, and chowder for supper, till you began to look for fish-bones coming through your clothes.", note="A whole island's economy has soaked into its milk. It's a joke, but it's also the first description in the book of what living entirely inside one industry does to a person—which is what the Pequod will be."),
 dict(quote="was found dead in my first floor back, with his harpoon in his side; ever since then I allow no boarders to take sich dangerous weepons in their rooms at night.", note="A suicide reduced to a house rule, delivered in the same breath as 'clam or cod for breakfast.' I find that more chilling than a solemn treatment would be. Also: it is the seed of the whole next chapter."),
])

D["ch16"] = dict(essay="""This is the chapter where the book's machinery gets bolted together, and I'm always surprised how much of it is comic. Yojo, the little black god, picks Ishmael to choose the ship—so that fate can arrive "for all the world as though it had turned out by chance." That parenthetical is the whole novel's theory of providence in one clause. Ishmael thinks he's freely choosing; the text is already smiling at him.

The Pequod herself gets the best paragraph. "A cannibal of a craft, tricking herself forth in the chased bones of her enemies"—she is a ship built out of what she kills, her tiller carved from a whale's jaw. And then that line I keep rereading: "A noble craft, but somehow a most melancholy! All noble things are touched with that." Melville drops a general law about nobility in the middle of a ship description, as if it were nothing.

Then Peleg and Bildad, the fighting Quakers. I love the lay negotiation as pure comedy—Bildad reading "Lay not up for yourselves treasures" while offering the 777th lay—but the joke has teeth. The ship is owned by "widows, fatherless children, and chancery wards," and Bildad uses those widows as the reason to underpay a sailor. That is capitalism speaking in scripture's voice, and Melville lets it be both funny and rotten. And buried in the middle, a paragraph that isn't about Bildad at all: the man "formed for noble tragedies," whose greatness is "but disease." We haven't met Ahab yet. This is his overture, hidden in a chapter about paperwork. Peleg's closing speech—"Ahab has his humanities!"—is the first time anyone in the book pleads for him, and it lands harder because Peleg is such a blusterer.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="for all the world as though it had turned out by chance", note="Yojo's plan is that Ishmael's free choice will feel free while being fated. This is a small comic sentence but it's the book's whole stance on predestination: the loom weaves, and you think you're holding the shuttle."),
 dict(quote="_Pequod_, you will no doubt remember, was the name of a celebrated tribe of Massachusetts Indians; now extinct as the ancient Medes.", note="The ship is named for a people who were massacred. 'You will no doubt remember' is Ishmael's dry way of noting that nobody remembers. A ship named for the exterminated, hunting a creature to extermination—Melville doesn't underline it, and he doesn't need to."),
 dict(quote="A cannibal of a craft, tricking herself forth in the chased bones of her enemies.", note="Two chapters ago Ishmael was afraid of sleeping next to a cannibal. Now the ship itself is one, dressed in the bones of what it eats. The word keeps migrating from the 'savage' to everything Christian and commercial around him."),
 dict(quote="A noble craft, but somehow a most melancholy! All noble things are touched with that.", note="A universal law tossed off inside a ship description. I don't know if it's true, but the book behaves as though it is—every noble thing in it, Ahab most of all, is shadowed."),
 dict(quote="Can’t ye see the world where you stand?", note="Peleg's test is brutal and true. Ishmael says he wants to see the world; Peleg sends him to look at the horizon and it's nothing but water. That's what he's signing up for: three years of the same nothing, which is either emptiness or everything."),
 dict(quote="Be sure of this, O young ambition, all mortal greatness is but disease.", note="This isn't about Bildad, the man being described. Melville interrupts himself to prophesy Ahab, whom we won't see for twelve more chapters. Read it twice: he calls the 'half wilful overruling morbidness' a qualification for greatness, not a flaw in it."),
 dict(quote="a man’s religion is one thing, and this practical world quite another. This world pays dividends.", note="The most economical indictment of comfortable piety I know. Bildad is a sworn foe of bloodshed who has spilled 'tuns upon tuns of leviathan gore,' and the reconciliation is simply: dividends. Melville isn't sneering exactly—he seems fascinated that it works."),
 dict(quote="“_Lay_ not up for yourselves treasures upon earth, where moth—”", note="Bildad is reading the one verse in scripture that puns on the word he's about to use to cheat Ishmael. The comedy is perfect and the theology is exact: the man quoting it has laid up treasures on earth his whole life."),
 dict(quote="He’s a grand, ungodly, god-like man, Captain Ahab", note="Peleg's phrase is the book's first attempt to describe Ahab and it's a contradiction held in one breath. 'Ungodly, god-like' will turn out to be precise: a man who defies God by trying to be one."),
 dict(quote="stricken, blasted, if he be, Ahab has his humanities!", note="A wife, a child, a moody good man who'll come round. Everything Peleg says here is true and none of it will matter. It's the last defense of Ahab offered by someone who loves him, before we meet him and it becomes impossible to defend him and impossible to look away."),
])

D["ch17"] = dict(essay="""I laughed out loud at this chapter and then felt caught. Ishmael opens with a genuinely generous paragraph—he respects everyone's religious obligations "never mind how comical," including ants worshipping a toad-stool and men bowing to a dead landlord's torso—and then spends the rest of the chapter unable to tolerate his friend sitting quietly on the floor for a day. The tolerance was easy in the abstract. The Ramadan, four feet from his bed, in the cold, drove him half mad.

The locked-door sequence is farce done with real craft: the chamber-maid's "La! la, ma'am!—Mistress! murder!", Mrs. Hussey with her mustard-pot and vinegar-cruet, the suicide sign she wants painted ("no suicides permitted here, and no smoking in the parlor"). Then the door bursts and there is Queequeg, "cool and self-collected," holding Yojo on his head like a man who has never once been in a hurry.

What I get out of the ending is how thoroughly Ishmael loses the argument. He delivers a lecture on hygiene and dyspepsia and the origins of hell in an undigested apple-dumpling—a lecture I half agree with—and Queequeg understands a third of it, is unmoved, and looks at him with "condescending concern and compassion." Melville lets the pagan have the last word, silently. And the throwaway about the feast where fifty enemies were cooked by evening is dropped in and abandoned with "No more, Queequeg." The book keeps refusing to make Queequeg safe.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="for we are all somehow dreadfully cracked about the head, and sadly need mending.", note="The book's most honest creed, and it comes from a man who is about to prove it by panicking over a locked door. Ishmael's theology: universal damage, universal mercy."),
 dict(quote="hell is an idea first born on an undigested apple-dumpling", note="Ishmael's whole philosophy of religion reduced to indigestion. It's a joke, but it's also a serious materialist claim about where the doctrine of eternal punishment comes from—and he offers it to a man who has eaten people, without irony."),
 dict(quote="He looked at me with a sort of condescending concern and compassion, as though he thought it a great pity that such a sensible young man should be so hopelessly lost to evangelical pagan piety.", note="The missionary position reversed. Every sentence Ishmael spoke could be turned around, and Queequeg turns it around without saying a word. Melville seems to relish giving the 'savage' the superior calm."),
 dict(quote="so that the landlady should not make much profit by reason of his Ramadan", note="Queequeg fasts all day and then eats a breakfast large enough to erase the innkeeper's savings. A tiny act of economic justice, and a reminder that he is shrewd, not merely picturesque."),
])

D["ch18"] = dict(essay="""A short chapter with one great speech and one great throw. Bildad wants Queequeg's papers—proof he's converted—and Ishmael improvises the "First Congregational Church" of "this whole worshipping world," to which everyone belongs and in which the differences are only "queer crotchets." Peleg calls it a sermon. It is one, and it's better than Father Mapple's in a way: it demands nothing of anyone but that they admit they're all in the same boat, which they are about to be, literally.

Then Queequeg answers with his harpoon instead of his mouth. The tar spot on the water, the iron sailing over Bildad's hat, "spos-ee him whale-e eye; why, dad whale dead." Bildad's theological objections evaporate at once; he'll take a pagan who can throw. The ninetieth lay follows. The book keeps showing that the market has no religion; competence is the only creed that actually gets enforced.

And the signature. "Quohog. his X mark." Peleg can't hold Queequeg's name, and Queequeg copies a tattoo from his own arm—a figure whose meaning, we will learn much later, even he doesn't know. His identity on the ship's articles is a wrong name and an unreadable sign. I find that quietly devastating. The last image is Bildad, having preached the fiery pit, stooping to save a scrap of tarred twine. That is the man entire.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="the great and everlasting First Congregation of this whole worshipping world; we all belong to that; only some of us cherish some queer crotchets no ways touching the grand belief", note="Ishmael's improvised universalism. He means it as a dodge to get Queequeg aboard, but it's also the most generous religious statement in the book, and Peleg's 'splice hands' makes it a sailor's covenant."),
 dict(quote="“Now,” said Queequeg, quietly hauling in the line, “spos-ee him whale-e eye; why, dad whale dead.”", note="Queequeg's rebuttal to the demand for conversion papers. The market doesn't care about his soul once it sees his arm. Note 'quietly'—he never performs; he just does the thing."),
 dict(quote="Quohog. his X mark.", note="A wrong name and a tattoo he copies from his own arm. This is how Queequeg exists in the ship's records: misnamed, marked with a sign nobody—including him—can read. Keep it in mind for the coffin chapter."),
 dict(quote="Pious harpooneers never make good voyagers—it takes the shark out of ’em", note="Peleg's counter-theology. Fear of hell makes you flinch from whales. He's arguing that salvation is bad for business, and the whole chapter agrees with him."),
 dict(quote="Now and then he stooped to pick up a patch, or save an end of tarred twine, which otherwise might have been wasted.", note="After all the fiery-pit preaching, this is Bildad's real religion: nothing wasted. Melville ends the chapter on it without comment, and it's the truest portrait of him in the book."),
])

D["ch19"] = dict(essay="""Elijah is the book's first outright prophet and Ishmael treats him exactly as I would: as a nuisance who might be a lunatic. What unsettles me on rereading is how much Elijah actually tells us—the three days Ahab lay like dead off Cape Horn, the fight before an altar in Santa, the silver calabash he spat into, the leg lost "according to the prophecy"—and how the book never explains any of it. These are fragments of a backstory that Melville deliberately leaves as fragments. The reader, like Ishmael, has to decide whether they're data or noise.

The "souls" exchange is my favorite bit. "Anything down there about your souls?"—as if the ship's articles might have a clause. Then: "A soul's a sort of a fifth wheel to a wagon." He's mocking, but he's also saying something true about what a whaleship asks of you. And then the pivot: He has enough soul "to make up for all deficiencies of that sort in other chaps." Ahab is not soulless; he's over-souled, and that's the danger.

Ishmael's final verdict—"a humbug"—arrives twice, and each time he has to work for it. He crosses the street to test whether Elijah is following. He is proud of his skepticism, and the book lets him keep it, because the alternative is to walk away from the ship, and nobody in this book ever walks away.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="A soul’s a sort of a fifth wheel to a wagon.", note="Delivered as a joke about sailors, it's really a description of the Pequod's economy: souls are surplus equipment. The ship will test whether that's true."),
 dict(quote="_He’s_ got enough, though, to make up for all deficiencies of that sort in other chaps", note="Ahab's problem is not too little soul but too much. Elijah says it first and says it best; every later description of Ahab is a footnote to this."),
 dict(quote="what’s signed, is signed; and what’s to be, will be; and then again, perhaps it won’t be, after all.", note="The prophet hedges his own prophecy. That 'perhaps it won't be' is what keeps Elijah from being a mere plot device: even he doesn't know if fate is fixed, and the book won't decide either."),
 dict(quote="It is the easiest thing in the world for a man to look as if he had a great secret in him.", note="Ishmael's skepticism, and it's a good sentence. But the book is full of men who look as if they have a great secret in them—Ahab most of all—and every one of them turns out to actually have one."),
 dict(quote="I pronounced him in my heart, a humbug.", note="He has to pronounce it twice, and cross a street to prove it. The insistence is the tell. Ishmael needs Elijah to be a humbug because otherwise he'd have to not sail."),
])

D["ch20"] = dict(essay="""A chapter about provisioning, and I think it's secretly about denial. The list is wonderful—pickles, quills, flannel for someone's rheumatic back, Aunt Charity bustling with an oil-ladle in one hand and a whaling lance in the other—and the joke about "spare everythings, almost, but a spare Captain and duplicate ship" is one of those Melville lines that only turns dark later, when you realize the lack of a spare captain is the plot.

But the paragraph that matters is the one where Ishmael admits he didn't want to look at his own doubt. He's signed on for three years under a man he has never seen, and "when a man suspects any wrong, it sometimes happens that if he be already involved in the matter, he insensibly strives to cover up his suspicions even from himself." That is as clear-eyed a sentence about sunk cost as I know. He said nothing "and tried to think nothing." Every crewman on the ship is doing the same thing, and the chapter shows you the mechanism before the voyage even starts.

Aunt Charity deserves her sentence. She is the only woman in the book who does anything, and what she does is make sure nothing is wanting—she owns "a score or two of well-saved dollars" in the ship, which means even her kindness is partly investment. Melville can't help noting it, and I like him for not pretending otherwise.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Hence, the spare boats, spare spars, and spare lines and harpoons, and spare everythings, almost, but a spare Captain and duplicate ship.", note="A logistics joke that becomes the plot. The ship carries redundancy for everything except the one part that will fail."),
 dict(quote="with a long oil-ladle in one hand, and a still longer whaling lance in the other", note="Aunt Charity, Quakeress, carrying the tools of both cooking and killing. Melville keeps putting domesticity and slaughter in the same hands; this is the book's honest picture of what the industry is."),
 dict(quote="when a man suspects any wrong, it sometimes happens that if he be already involved in the matter, he insensibly strives to cover up his suspicions even from himself.", note="The psychology of the whole crew, stated before they sail. Ishmael can name it and still not act on it—which is what makes the naming honest rather than wise."),
 dict(quote="I said nothing, and tried to think nothing.", note="Six words, and they're the hinge of the chapter. Not thinking is a choice here, and it's the one that ships him."),
])

D["ch21"] = dict(essay="""The strangest chapter in the Nantucket section, and the one I understood least on first reading. Elijah's questions—did you see men going toward the ship? can you find them now?—are about the shadowy figures who'll turn out to be Fedallah's crew, and Ishmael half-sees them "too dim to be sure," then talks himself out of it. Melville is doing something bold: he gives you a supernatural hint and lets the narrator suppress it in real time. "I beat the thing down."

Then the tone swings completely. Queequeg sits on a sleeping rigger's rear end because "my country way," and explains that in his land the lower orders are fattened for use as ottomans, "much better than those garden-chairs which are convertible into walking-sticks." He flourishes his tomahawk over the man's head: "Perry easy, kill-e." I don't know how to feel about this and I think that's the intended condition. Ishmael's "it's grinding the face of the poor" is Isaiah, and it's a joke, and it's also an accurate description of the labor system the ship runs on.

And the last line: Ahab "remained invisibly enshrined within his cabin." Enshrined. The crew comes aboard in twos and threes and the captain is a relic in a reliquary. Twenty-one chapters in, we still haven't seen him. The withholding is beginning to feel like a pressure, which is exactly what it's for.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Shan’t see ye again very soon, I guess; unless it’s before the Grand Jury.", note="Elijah's last words, and they assume a trial—someone will have to answer for what happens. The Epilogue is that testimony. He knows Ishmael is the witness before Ishmael does."),
 dict(quote="But I beat the thing down", note="Ishmael suppressing a perception in real time. The dim figures were real. This is the book's method with the supernatural: shown, doubted, ignored, and later vindicated."),
 dict(quote="it’s grinding the face of the poor.", note="Isaiah 3:15, applied to Queequeg sitting on a sleeping rigger. A joke, a Biblical allusion, and a literal description of how the ship's economy works, all in one line."),
 dict(quote="the king, chiefs, and great people generally, were in the custom of fattening some of the lower orders for ottomans", note="Queequeg's furniture anecdote is grotesque and cheerful and I can't tell how seriously Melville wants it taken. What I notice is that Ishmael doesn't recoil—he just tells Queequeg to get off because the man is heavy."),
 dict(quote="Meanwhile Captain Ahab remained invisibly enshrined within his cabin.", note="'Enshrined'—the word for a saint's relics. The crew is being assembled around an unseen holy object. The book has spent twenty-one chapters not showing him, and this word explains why."),
])

D["ch22"] = dict(essay="""Sailing on Christmas Day, and Melville makes almost nothing of the holiday except the title and one phrase—"a short, cold Christmas." The chapter is instead about the two old owners who cannot let go. Peleg kicks Ishmael in the rear (his "first kick") while Bildad sings psalms at the windlass over a crew roaring about the girls in Booble Alley. It's the sacred and profane heaving on the same rope, and the anchor comes up anyway.

What surprised me is how tender it becomes. Bildad, the skinflint who offered the 777th lay, lingers on deck, "looked everywhere and nowhere," and grips Peleg's hand by lantern-light. Peleg, the blusterer, has a tear "when the lantern came too near." And then Bildad's farewell speech, which is one of the funniest and saddest things in the book: prayers and cheese in the same breath, "beware of fornication" and "be careful with the butter—twenty cents the pound it was." He cannot separate his love for the ship from his ledger, and I don't think Melville wants him to. That is what love looks like in a man like that.

The hymn matters. "Sweet fields beyond the swelling flood" is Watts on the Jordan and Canaan, and Ishmael hears it as pure hope—"many a pleasant haven in store." He's wrong, and the book knows it, and the final sentence corrects him: three "heavy-hearted cheers," and "blindly plunged like fate into the lone Atlantic." Blindly. Like fate. The voyage begins with the ship's own eyes shut.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Nevertheless, not three days previous, Bildad had told them that no profane songs would be allowed on board the Pequod", note="The rule lasts exactly until the anchor needs raising. Bildad sings psalms; the men sing Booble Alley; the work gets done. The book's picture of how religion and labor actually coexist."),
 dict(quote="That was my first kick.", note="Four words, and a whole history of hierarchy at sea. Ishmael counts it like a rite of passage. The merchant service, we were told, would be damned; this is the whaling service's welcome."),
 dict(quote="whose freezing spray cased us in ice, as in polished armor", note="The first image of the ship at sea, and she's already armored—for a fight, not a fishing trip. Melville's similes keep telling you what kind of book this is before the plot does."),
 dict(quote="Never did those sweet words sound more sweetly to me than then. They were full of hope and fruition.", note="Ishmael hearing Watts's hymn about Canaan as a promise. The reader who knows the ending hears it as a hymn about crossing the river of death—which is what it actually is."),
 dict(quote="looked everywhere and nowhere; and at last, mechanically coiling a rope upon its pin", note="Bildad unable to leave. The 'mechanically coiling a rope' is the tell: a man whose feelings have nowhere to go except into his hands. Melville is so good at old men."),
 dict(quote="Be careful with the butter—twenty cents the pound it was, and mind ye, if—”", note="Cut off mid-sentence, still doing accounts. It would be cruel satire if the previous paragraph hadn't shown him weeping. Instead it's the most loving thing he knows how to say."),
 dict(quote="we gave three heavy-hearted cheers, and blindly plunged like fate into the lone Atlantic.", note="'Blindly' and 'like fate' in the same clause. The ship sets out sightless, and the narrator who will survive to tell it already knows. This sentence is the whole novel's trajectory compressed."),
])

D["ch23"] = dict(essay="""Six paragraphs, and Melville calls it a "six-inch chapter" and "the stoneless grave of Bulkington." I think it's the most concentrated piece of philosophy in the book, and it's built around a sailor we saw for one page at the Spouter-Inn and will never see again. Bulkington is at the helm on the first night out, just back from four years at sea, unable to stay on land. "The land seemed scorching to his feet."

The argument runs: a ship in a gale near a lee shore must flee the harbor that would save it, because "one touch of land, though it but graze the keel, would make her shudder through and through." Safety is what kills you. Then the turn to the mind: "all deep, earnest thinking is but the intrepid effort of the soul to keep the open independence of her sea." Every comfort—hearthstone, supper, friends—is the shore, and thinking honestly means sailing away from all of it into "the howling infinite."

I'm not sure I believe this. It's the creed of a man who cannot stay home, dressed as a universal truth, and Melville half-knows it—that's why it's an apostrophe, a shout at Bulkington rather than a proof. But the phrase "in landlessness alone resides highest truth, shoreless, indefinite as God" has stayed with me for years. It says that certainty is a lee shore. Whether or not that's true, it's the book's own method: it will refuse, chapter after chapter, to land.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Wonderfullest things are ever the unmentionable; deep memories yield no epitaphs; this six-inch chapter is the stoneless grave of Bulkington.", note="Melville buries a character in a chapter, and tells you he's doing it. A man too large to write about gets the shortest chapter. I think this is the book's most honest admission that its biggest subjects can't be said, only pointed at."),
 dict(quote="in the port is safety, comfort, hearthstone, supper, warm blankets, friends, all that’s kind to our mortalities.", note="He lists everything good in life, and then says the ship must fly from it. The list is not sneered at—'all that's kind to our mortalities' is tender. That's what makes the rejection cost something."),
 dict(quote="all deep, earnest thinking is but the intrepid effort of the soul to keep the open independence of her sea", note="Thinking as seamanship: not reaching a conclusion but keeping off the rocks of one. This is the line that justifies the book's whole digressive shape. Every cetology chapter is Ishmael crowding sail off shore."),
 dict(quote="But as in landlessness alone resides highest truth, shoreless, indefinite as God—so, better is it to perish in that howling infinite, than be ingloriously dashed upon the lee, even if that were safety!", note="A creed I keep arguing with. It equates God with indefiniteness and truth with having no ground under you. It's also, quietly, a prediction of how the book will end—perishing in the infinite rather than reaching port."),
 dict(quote="Up from the spray of thy ocean-perishing—straight up, leaps thy apotheosis!", note="Bulkington is deified in advance of his death, which we never see. A pattern: Melville's heroes are made gods by drowning. Watch for it with Ahab."),
])

D["ch24"] = dict(essay="""Ishmael as lawyer, and I find him more persuasive than he has any right to be. The case for whaling's dignity is argued in a courtroom voice—"But this is not the half; look again"—and it mixes real facts (the Dunkirk Nantucketers, the British bounties, seven hundred ships and eighteen thousand men) with outrageous claims (the whale-ship liberated Peru; the whale-ship is "the true mother" of Australia). What I take from it is that he half-believes the outrageous parts, and that the mix is the point: whaling really did open the Pacific, and the industry really was, as he says, everywhere and honored nowhere.

The best sentence is the one about butchers. "Butchers we are, that is true. But butchers, also, and butchers of the bloodiest badge have been all Martial Commanders whom the world invariably delights to honor." That's a fair hit. And then the lamps: the whole world burns sperm oil "as before so many shrines, to our glory." Every candle is a tribute to a trade nobody will admit to admiring.

The chapter ends by turning personal in a way that makes me sit up. If there is any "undiscovered prime thing" in him, if his creditors find "precious MSS." in his desk, the credit goes to whaling, "for a whale-ship was my Yale College and my Harvard." Melville had been to sea and not to college; the sentence is his own. It's also a warning about the book you're holding: it's going to be a whale-ship, not a lecture hall.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="For what are the comprehensible terrors of man compared with the interlinked terrors and wonders of God!", note="The argument for whaling's dignity turns theological here: soldiers face what men do, whalemen face what God made. It's a boast, but it's also the book's real subject announced in a sentence."),
 dict(quote="for almost all the tapers, lamps, and candles that burn round the globe, burn, as before so many shrines, to our glory!", note="The whole lit world as an altar to whaling. There's a bitterness under the pride: the trade is everywhere in daily life and honored nowhere. That's still true of most trades."),
 dict(quote="Ah, the world! Oh, the world!", note="After a paragraph of listing what anonymous Nantucketers did that Cook gets credit for, Ishmael just sighs at it. The two exclamations are the sound of an argument giving up and becoming grief."),
 dict(quote="Drive down your hat in presence of the Czar, and take it off to Queequeg!", note="The chapter's real politics. All the imperial and royal name-dropping has been leading here: the harpooneer outranks the emperor. Melville's democracy is sincere, and it's going to be argued more solemnly in two chapters."),
 dict(quote="for a whale-ship was my Yale College and my Harvard.", note="Melville's own biography surfacing through Ishmael. Whatever this book turns out to be, he's telling you where its education came from—and asking you to weigh that against the universities."),
])

D["ch25"] = dict(essay="""Three paragraphs of pure mischief, and I love that Melville kept it in. Having argued whaling's dignity with facts, the advocate now adds "a not unreasonable surmise": kings are anointed with oil at their coronations, and what oil could it possibly be but sperm oil? Therefore "we whalemen supply your kings and queens with coronation stuff!"

The route to that punchline goes through hair-oil. A grown man who uses it "has probably got a quoggy spot in him somewhere"—one of those Melville aside-verdicts that feels like a friend leaning over at a party. And the king's head is oiled "even as a head of salad," and perhaps "with a view of making its interior run well, as they anoint machinery." Monarchy reduced to lubrication in a paragraph.

I don't think this is filler. It's the book teaching you its manners: after every earnest passage there's a wink, and the wink is often where the real subversion lives. A sailor's oil on a sovereign's skull—the lowest trade touching the highest office. That's the same joke as "take it off to Queequeg," told sideways.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Certain I am, however, that a king’s head is solemnly oiled at his coronation, even as a head of salad.", note="Monarchy as salad dressing. The comic register is doing serious democratic work: nothing about a king's head is different in kind from a lettuce."),
 dict(quote="a mature man who uses hair-oil, unless medicinally, that man has probably got a quoggy spot in him somewhere.", note="An unprovoked verdict on hair-oil, delivered with the confidence of a maxim. I include it because it's the voice of the book at its most companionable—opinions about everything, delivered as if to a friend."),
 dict(quote="Think of that, ye loyal Britons! we whalemen supply your kings and queens with coronation stuff!", note="The whole 'Advocate' argument ends on a shrug of a joke. That's the rhythm to learn: earnest, then absurd, and the absurd part often says the true thing more plainly."),
])

D["ch26"] = dict(essay="""The first "Knights and Squires" is really two chapters: a portrait of Starbuck, and then a hymn that bursts out of the portrait as if Melville couldn't hold it. The Starbuck half is precise. His thinness is "merely the condensation of the man"; his courage is "not a sentiment; but a thing simply useful," rationed like beef and bread. "I will have no man in my boat who is not afraid of a whale." That's the sanest sentence anyone speaks in this book, and it's spoken by the man who will lose.

Because Melville tells you now that he'll lose. Starbuck's bravery can stand up to seas and whales but not to "those more terrific, because more spiritual terrors, which sometimes menace you from the concentrating brow of an enraged and mighty man." I find this the book's most painful foreshadowing, because it's delivered with such respect. Starbuck isn't weak; he's reasonable, and reason is what fails in the presence of Ahab.

Then the hymn. Melville stops narrating and begs: if he gives "meanest mariners, and renegades and castaways" tragic dignity, "bear me out in it, thou just Spirit of Equality." God as "the centre and circumference of all democracy," who chose Bunyan the convict, Cervantes the maimed, Jackson from the pebbles. I'm never sure whether to be moved or embarrassed, and I've settled on moved. It's the book's manifesto: that a whaleman can carry tragic weight, that "the arm that wields a pick or drives a spike" has dignity that needs no robes. Everything the book does with Ahab depends on this paragraph being true.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="It was merely the condensation of the man.", note="Starbuck described as reduced to essentials, no water lost to feeling. Melville's physical descriptions are always moral: this is what a reasonable man looks like when there's no excess to spare."),
 dict(quote="“I will have no man in my boat,” said Starbuck, “who is not afraid of a whale.”", note="The most sensible line in the book, and Melville immediately glosses it: an utterly fearless man is more dangerous than a coward. The book is going to be commanded by exactly that man."),
 dict(quote="courage was one of the great staple outfits of the ship, like her beef and her bread, and not to be foolishly wasted", note="Courage as provision, with a finite supply. It's the practical man's ethics, and it explains why Starbuck won't lower after sundown—and why he'll be helpless when someone demands the whole store at once."),
 dict(quote="What doom was his own father’s? Where, in the bottomless deeps, could he find the torn limbs of his brother?", note="Two questions, no answers. Starbuck's caution has a graveyard under it. Melville grants him this history in one line and never mentions it again."),
 dict(quote="yet cannot withstand those more terrific, because more spiritual terrors, which sometimes menace you from the concentrating brow of an enraged and mighty man.", note="The verdict on Starbuck, delivered before Ahab has spoken a word. His failure is announced with something like tenderness. Reason is brave against nature and helpless against will."),
 dict(quote="man, in the ideal, is so noble and so sparkling, such a grand and glowing creature, that over any ignominious blemish in him all his fellows should run to throw their costliest robes.", note="Melville's humanism stated as a plea. He's asking the reader in advance to cover Starbuck's coming shame. I find it disarming that he cares this much for a character's dignity."),
 dict(quote="that democratic dignity which, on all hands, radiates without end from God; Himself! The great God absolute! The centre and circumference of all democracy!", note="God as the source of equality, not of hierarchy. This is 1851 American theology at its boldest—and it's the license Melville claims for making a tragedy out of a whaleman."),
 dict(quote="Thou who didst pick up Andrew Jackson from the pebbles; who didst hurl him upon a war-horse; who didst thunder him higher than a throne!", note="Jackson, of all people, as proof of democratic election by God. It dates the passage and it doesn't matter; the point is the shape—greatness hurled up from the bottom—which is also the shape of the book's own ambition."),
])

D["ch27"] = dict(essay="""Stubb and Flask and the three harpooneers, and then a paragraph that quietly becomes one of the book's most important. Stubb first: "Long usage had, for this Stubb, converted the jaws of death into an easy chair." He smokes constantly, and Ishmael suggests the tobacco works "as a sort of disinfecting agent" against the "nameless miseries of the numberless mortals who have died exhaling" the air. I've never been able to read that without thinking it's true of most cheerfulness—a filter against the atmosphere. Flask is simpler: he thinks the whale is "a species of magnified mouse," a man with no reverence and therefore no fear.

Tashtego and Daggoo are drawn with a mix of genuine admiration and the racial vocabulary of 1851, and I don't want to pretend that's not there. What I notice is the direction of the comparisons: a white man before Daggoo is "a white flag come to beg truce of a fortress," and Flask, his officer, "looked like a chess-man beside him." The hierarchy of the ship and the hierarchy of the men point opposite ways, and Melville says so: Americans provide "the brains, the rest of the world as generously supplying the muscles." That's an observation, not an endorsement, and it's still accurate about a lot of industries.

Then the word: Isolatoes. "Not acknowledging the common continent of men, but each Isolato living on a separate continent of his own. Yet now, federated along one keel." An "Anacharsis Clootz deputation"—the delegation of all humanity that showed up at the French Revolution—sailing to "lay the world's grievances before that bar from which not very many of them ever come back." And then, out of nowhere, Pip. "He never did—oh, no! he went before." The book tells you, in a chapter about crew rosters, who is going to die first and how he'll be received in heaven.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Long usage had, for this Stubb, converted the jaws of death into an easy chair.", note="Stubb's whole character. Not brave, not cowardly—habituated. Melville seems to suspect that most people survive terror this way, and to half admire it and half find it appalling."),
 dict(quote="Stubb’s tobacco smoke might have operated as a sort of disinfecting agent.", note="Cheerfulness as a filter against the exhaled misery of everyone who ever died. It's a joke about pipes, and it's one of the book's saddest theories of temperament."),
 dict(quote="the wondrous whale was but a species of magnified mouse, or at least water-rat", note="Flask's error is the absence of wonder. The book will argue that seeing the whale as a mouse is a failure of perception, not of courage—and that it's Flask, not Ahab, who's simply wrong about what a whale is."),
 dict(quote="a white man standing before him seemed a white flag come to beg truce of a fortress.", note="Melville reverses the ship's hierarchy in a simile. Daggoo is the third mate's subordinate and the third mate is a chess-piece beside him. The book keeps noting that rank and stature don't line up."),
 dict(quote="the native American liberally provides the brains, the rest of the world as generously supplying the muscles.", note="A plain description of how American labor was organized in 1851, in whaling and canals and railroads alike. The irony in 'liberally' and 'generously' is Ishmael's; the arrangement is real."),
 dict(quote="_Isolatoes_ too, I call such, not acknowledging the common continent of men, but each _Isolato_ living on a separate continent of his own. Yet now, federated along one keel", note="Melville coins a word for the crew and it's the book's social theory: islands, temporarily a nation. 'Federated along one keel' is America, and it's also every group of strangers who've ever had to work together."),
 dict(quote="Black Little Pip—he never did—oh, no! he went before. Poor Alabama boy!", note="Pip's fate announced in an aside, in a chapter about the crew list, hundreds of pages early. 'Called a coward here, hailed a hero there'—the book's judgment on him is delivered before we meet him. Nothing in Moby-Dick is a surprise; everything is a fulfillment."),
])

D["ch28"] = dict(essay="""Twenty-eight chapters, and here he is. I think the delay is the most important structural choice in the book, because by now Ahab is made of other people's sentences—Peleg's "ungodly, god-like," Elijah's calabash, the word "enshrined"—and the actual man has to be seen through all of them. "Reality outran apprehension; Captain Ahab stood upon his quarter-deck." Melville doesn't describe him for two more sentences; the first thing is just presence.

Then the description, which is all fire and bronze and lightning. He looks "like a man cut away from the stake, when the fire has overrunningly wasted all the limbs without consuming them." The scar is a lightning-seam down a tree that leaves it "greenly alive, but branded." The crew argue about whether he was born with it. This is a man described as a thing that's been struck. And he stands in an auger hole bored into the deck—literally pegged to the ship, "one arm elevated, and holding by a shroud," looking forward with "unsurrenderable wilfulness." Every physical detail is a metaphor and every metaphor is physical.

The phrase that stops me is "a crucifixion in his face." Ishmael doesn't say he looks like Christ; he says the suffering is on his face the way it's on a crucifix—"the nameless regal overbearing dignity of some mighty woe." Peleg said Ahab has his humanities. This paragraph says the humanities are what's being crucified. And then, at the end, the spring weather draws out "the faint blossom of a look, which, in any other man, would have soon flowered out in a smile." In any other man. Melville lets you feel how close Ahab is to being ordinary, and how far.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Reality outran apprehension; Captain Ahab stood upon his quarter-deck.", note="After twenty-eight chapters of rumor, six words of fact. No description yet—just that he's there. The delay has turned Ahab into a pressure, and this sentence is the pressure breaking."),
 dict(quote="He looked like a man cut away from the stake, when the fire has overrunningly wasted all the limbs without consuming them", note="Ahab's first description is of a man who has been burned and not destroyed. Everything after—the bronze, the lightning-scar—keeps this note: he's what's left after something elemental went through him."),
 dict(quote="leaving the tree still greenly alive, but branded.", note="The scar as lightning down a tree trunk. What I notice is 'greenly alive'—the image insists that the strike didn't kill him, which is somehow worse than if it had. He's a living thing marked by a force."),
 dict(quote="then, whoever should do that last office for the dead, would find a birth-mark on him from crown to sole.", note="The Manxman's rumor: the scar goes all the way down; it's not a wound but a nature. The book never resolves whether Ahab was struck or born this way, and that's the question about him."),
 dict(quote="His bone leg steadied in that hole; one arm elevated, and holding by a shroud; Captain Ahab stood erect", note="An auger hole bored into the deck for his ivory leg. He is physically pegged to the ship. The image of a man who can't move and won't be moved, in one detail of carpentry."),
 dict(quote="moody stricken Ahab stood before them with a crucifixion in his face", note="Not 'like Christ'—'a crucifixion in his face.' The suffering is the point, not the sanctity. Melville is careful: this is a man being tortured on something, and we don't yet know what."),
 dict(quote="as ever all clouds choose the loftiest peaks to pile themselves upon.", note="Ahab's gloom as weather on a mountain. The simile makes his suffering a function of his height. The book will keep insisting that his misery and his greatness are the same fact."),
 dict(quote="More than once did he put forth the faint blossom of a look, which, in any other man, would have soon flowered out in a smile.", note="'In any other man.' Melville shows you how near Ahab is to ordinary warmth and then closes the door. Rereading, this is the sentence I find saddest in the chapter."),
])

D["ch29"] = dict(essay="""The title is a stage direction, and it announces what the book is about to become: a play. From here Ahab will get soliloquies, entrances, and exits, and the prose will slip into blank verse whenever he opens his mouth. The chapter's opening is almost drunk on weather—days like "crystal goblets of Persian sherbet," nights like "haughty dames in jewelled velvets"—and then the sentence that turns it: all this beauty "inward turned upon the soul," and "more and more they wrought on Ahab's texture." Pleasant weather makes him worse. That tells you everything about the direction he's pointed.

Then Ahab and Stubb. Stubb, who converted death into an easy chair, makes one tentative suggestion—muffle the ivory heel with tow so the men can sleep—and Ahab calls him a dog and threatens to "clear the world of thee." It's the first time we hear Ahab speak, and what he says is: "Below to thy nightly grave; where such as ye sleep between shrouds, to use ye to the filling one at last." He can't mention a bed without a coffin. Even the cabin scuttle is "going down into one's tomb."

Stubb's monologue afterward is my favorite thing in the chapter, because it's an ordinary man trying to think and finding he can't. Strike him or pray for him? "Think not, is my eleventh commandment; and sleep when you can, is my twelfth." And the detail about Ahab's pillow "a sort of frightful hot, as though a baked brick had been on it," from the steward—the crew are already collecting evidence about their captain, the way you'd watch a fire you can't put out. "Damn me, but all things are queer, come to think of 'em. But that's against my principles." That's most of us, and Stubb knows it, and goes back to bed.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="Inward they turned upon the soul, especially when the still mild hours of eve came on", note="Beautiful weather makes Ahab worse. Everything the world offers as consolation turns inward on him and becomes fuel. This is the first clear sign that his condition isn't something the voyage can cure."),
 dict(quote="“It feels like going down into one’s tomb,”—he would mutter to himself—“for an old captain like me to be descending this narrow scuttle, to go to my grave-dug berth.”", note="Ahab's first quoted words in the book, and they're about his own burial. He can't go to bed without rehearsing death. The tomb-scuttle image will return at the very end."),
 dict(quote="Some considering touch of humanity was in him", note="He muffles his own step so the mates can sleep. Melville keeps giving you these grains of Ahab's decency, and they're never enough to change anything—which is the tragedy's exact shape."),
 dict(quote="Below to thy nightly grave; where such as ye sleep between shrouds, to use ye to the filling one at last.—Down, dog, and kennel!", note="Stubb asks for quiet and gets told that his hammock is practice for his coffin. 'Shrouds' means both rigging and burial cloth. Ahab's first speech is a pun on death, delivered as an insult."),
 dict(quote="somehow, now, I don’t well know whether to go back and strike him, or—what’s that?—down here on my knees and pray for him?", note="Stubb's confusion is the crew's whole relationship to Ahab: rage and reverence with nothing in between. That he's never prayed before and thinks of it now is the chapter's real event."),
 dict(quote="and the pillow a sort of frightful hot, as though a baked brick had been on it", note="Evidence gathered by a steward. The crew are already investigating Ahab like a fever. This detail is more frightening than any speech because it's so domestic."),
 dict(quote="Think not, is my eleventh commandment; and sleep when you can, is my twelfth", note="Stubb's creed, and it's the opposite of Bulkington's in 'The Lee Shore.' One man keeps the open sea of thought; the other refuses to think on principle. The book respects both and follows neither."),
 dict(quote="Damn me, but all things are queer, come to think of ’em. But that’s against my principles.", note="Stubb touches the edge of the book's vertigo—everything is strange if you look—and backs away. I find this line very funny and a little heartbreaking. He's right, and he can't afford to be."),
])

D["ch30"] = dict(essay="""One page. Ahab lights his pipe at the binnacle lamp, sits on his ivory stool—"a Khan of the plank, and a king of the sea"—and finds that smoking no longer soothes. The throne of narwhale tusks is the book at its most kingly; the pipe going into the sea is the book at its most exact. Everything gentle in him is being discarded, one object at a time. Last chapter it was sleep. This chapter it's tobacco.

What I love is the reasoning. He's been "ignorantly smoking to windward all the while"—the smoke blowing back into his own face—"with such nervous whiffs, as if, like the dying whale, my final jets were the strongest and fullest of trouble." He compares himself to the animal he's hunting, at the moment of its death, without seeming to notice. The pipe is "meant for sereneness, to send up mild white vapors among mild white hairs, not among torn iron-grey locks like mine." He knows what peace looks like and knows he's not eligible for it.

Then the fire hissing in the waves and the ship shooting past "the bubble the sinking pipe made." That's the whole tragedy in one image: something that could have comforted him, extinguished by his own hand, and the ship already gone by. Stubb kept a whole rack of pipes to disinfect the air of mortality. Ahab throws his one away. That's the difference between them, and it's a difference of about two pages.""",
annotations=[
 dict(quote="For a Khan of the plank, and a king of the sea, and a great lord of Leviathans was Ahab.", note="The narwhale-tusk throne makes him a sea-king in the Norse line, and Melville means it. The book is going to insist that a whaleman can be royalty, and this is the coronation—right before he throws away the one thing that eased him."),
 dict(quote="as if, like the dying whale, my final jets were the strongest and fullest of trouble.", note="Ahab compares his own smoke to the death-spout of a whale, and doesn't hear what he's said. He's already identifying with the hunted thing's death. The hunter and the prey keep collapsing into each other in this book."),
 dict(quote="This thing that is meant for sereneness, to send up mild white vapors among mild white hairs, not among torn iron-grey locks like mine.", note="He knows exactly what serenity is for and rules himself out of it. The most self-aware line Ahab speaks before the quarter-deck, and it's about a pipe."),
 dict(quote="The fire hissed in the waves; the same instant the ship shot by the bubble the sinking pipe made.", note="Comfort discarded, and the ship past it before it's sunk. Melville does a whole tragedy in a sentence about a bubble. Stubb keeps a rack of pipes; Ahab throws his only one into the sea. That's the two men in one gesture."),
])

json.dump(D, open('commentary_2.json','w'), ensure_ascii=False, indent=1)
print('written', len(D))
